= Parkinson's law — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''Parkinson's law''' is the observation that work can grow to occupy the time available for it. C. Northcote Parkinson introduced the phrase in a humorous 1955 essay that also criticized the tendency of administrative offices to grow. It is a memorable warning about work and bureaucracy, '''not a scientific rule that always predicts how long a task will take'''.</onlyinclude>

== An everyday example ==
Suppose a person has two weeks to prepare a short presentation. They might spend much of the time revising slides and adding details. With only two days, they might instead produce a shorter presentation. That does not show the two-day version is as accurate or well checked: deadlines can change both effort and quality.

== What Parkinson was criticizing ==
His essay also discussed offices hiring more officials and creating additional correspondence even when their original workload was not growing. That organizational claim is different from the everyday deadline saying.

== How to use the idea ==
Ask whether each added meeting, revision, or reporting step improves the result. Set a realistic scope and leave time for important checks. Do not assume that faster work is automatically better.

== Important caution ==
The essay was satire using selected historical examples. It did not prove that every organization expands at a fixed rate or that every task fills every deadline.

